<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The first project in the nightly lane, and the reason the lane exists.

      ⚠ ONE CLASS, EIGHT MINUTES, ON A CHAIN THAT WAS ALREADY OVER BUDGET. KubeVirtOnAnEmptyCluster
      installs openebs-localpv, CDI and KubeVirt through charts/bundle/install.sh onto a fresh k3s,
      imports an image through charts/managed/image, provisions a disk through charts/managed/disk,
      applies charts/managed/virtual-machine against KubeVirt's real webhooks and reads the guest
      Running under KVM. Measured at 7 m 03 s to 8 m 26 s on a ten-CPU host. It landed in
      test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance, which is per-PR, and the review of #28 asked what
      that costs: gate.yml's `test` job runs every cluster-backed suite one at a time (build/Build.Test.cs
      § ClusterBackedSuiteDegree), that chain took 26 m 16 s on the runner on 2026-09-15 — read off the
      job's own log, against a 25-minute budget pr.yml enforces and a 30-minute job timeout — and this
      class would have put it past the timeout on every PR.

      ⚠ THE SUFFIX IS THE LANE. Directory.Build.props § Project role detection and build/Build.Test.cs
      § SuiteOwning both classify by suffix: `.Nightly` builds as a test host and is owned by
      `TestNightly`, which nightly.yml's `slow-suites` job runs on the runner's own Docker daemon.
      It is NOT `.Conformance`, which `Test` would run on every PR, and NOT `.Cluster.Conformance`,
      which carries the same per-PR meaning in sixteen other assemblies. charts/bundle/README.md
      § Verification used to say this lane did not exist and that a Testcontainers suite moved to
      nightly "would be run by nothing at all"; that was true of the three deployment-driven targets
      and is the sentence this project retires.

      ⚠ WHAT MOVED AND WHAT STAYED. The cert-manager, openebs-localpv and cloudnative-pg classes stay in
      test/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance and stay per-PR: together they cost under five
      minutes and are the install mechanism's own proof. What moved is the one class whose cost is the
      operators it installs rather than the script that installs them. EmptyClusterFixture and
      BundleInstaller stay where they were and are referenced from here, so this project starts its
      k3s the same way and takes the same ClusterSlot permit as its sibling.

      ⚠ THE DAEMON-FREE COMPANION MOVED WITH IT. KubeVirtComponentInstaller reads the installer's dry
      run and needs no cluster; without it a run of this assembly on a machine with no Docker daemon
      would skip its only test and report "Zero tests ran", which the minimum-expected-tests switch
      fails. The same shape CertManagerComponentInstaller gives the sibling.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.K3s"/>
        <!-- The raw client, for the half of every assertion here that is deliberately NOT our code. -->
        <PackageReference Include="KubernetesClient"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          For EmptyClusterFixture and BundleInstaller — the fixture that starts one k3s per class under
          ClusterSlot, and the runner of install.sh. Both are that project's and are not copied here,
          for the reason its own csproj gives about ClusterInfrastructure: a second spelling of a
          lock-file name is how two suites come to hold two clusters.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance/CyberCloud.Bundle.Cluster.Conformance.csproj"/>
        <!--
          ⚠ ONE PROVIDER'S .Contracts, FOR TWO READINGS. KubeVirtOnAnEmptyCluster hands the
          VirtualMachine a real KubeVirt wrote to VirtualMachines.ReadinessOf and VirtualMachines.Matches,
          so the sentence the reconciler would put in a tenant's InProgress message and the comparison
          that decides Converged are both asserted against an object the mutating webhook has been
          through. No reconciler runs here; the objects are applied by this test's own kubectl from the
          charts, exactly as CloudNativePgOnAnEmptyCluster applies its Cluster.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Providers/CyberCloud.Providers.Compute/CyberCloud.Providers.Compute.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.Compute.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
